# Development

Install this checkout with Zed's `zed: install dev extension` command. Edit files
in `themes/`. If changes do not appear, run `zed: reload extensions` from the
command palette. If title or status bars retain stale colors, run
`workspace: reload`; this was required during macOS 27 visual review.
Do not edit the installed copy of another extension.

## Validation

```sh
bun test
uvx --from check-jsonschema check-jsonschema --schemafile https://zed.dev/schema/themes/v0.2.0.json themes/*.json
```

The Bun tests check color syntax, paired appearances, opaque content surfaces,
syntax contrast (including cursor selections), chrome contrast over black/white
backdrops, focus visibility, and terminal color semantics. Schema validation needs
network access. Neither check proves native rendering or accessibility behavior.

## Visual review

Select each Glass appearance and inspect `test/test.js`, `test/test.c`, and
`test/test.md`. These are intentionally imperfect visual fixtures, not test suites.
Check the project panel, tabs, command palette, search results, diagnostics,
terminal ANSI colors, keyboard focus, and an inactive window. Compare light and
dark wallpapers, then test the README's opaque override. Verify system Reduce
Transparency and Increase Contrast behavior in the installed Zed version.

## Native Liquid Glass on macOS 27 — proposed GPUI work

This is an implementation outline for a separate Zed/GPUI change, not a feature
of this theme. The current extension supplies colors and requests whole-window
blur. It cannot insert native views, change layout geometry, or install shaders.
Full native glass currently requires a custom Zed build or an upstream change;
there is no theme-extension rendering hook to enable it.

### Existing boundary

Zed's macOS backend implements `set_background_appearance` by adjusting renderer
transparency and adding/removing a native blur view beneath its GPUI view. That
whole-window backdrop is distinct from per-control Liquid Glass. See the
[macOS window backend](https://github.com/zed-industries/zed/blob/main/crates/gpui_macos/src/window.rs).

Apple provides [`NSGlassEffectView`](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appkit/nsglasseffectview),
introduced in macOS 26 and available on macOS 27. A macOS 27-only API or a SwiftUI
rewrite is not required to investigate native glass: Rust can call AppKit through
Objective-C bindings. Use an SDK exposing the APIs, compatible bindings, and
runtime availability checks; preserve the existing path on unsupported systems.

### Smallest useful prototype

1. Add one opt-in native glass toolbar region in the macOS backend. Keep editor,
   terminal, and reading surfaces opaque. Define its bounds, clipping, lifetime,
   appearance, and relationship to `NSWindow` and its content view.
2. Prove native/Metal composition before expanding the design. Arbitrary GPUI
   elements are drawn content, not independently hosted AppKit views; wrapping
   the entire GPUI canvas in a glass view does not give each toolbar or sidebar
   its own material. Establish how the intended backdrop reaches the native
   effect, how foreground content stays above it, and how overlapping regions
   are ordered. This may require separate native content hosts or changes to
   GPUI composition; the workable bridge remains to be prototyped. Follow Apple's
   [`contentView` contract](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appkit/nsglasseffectview/contentview).
3. Synchronize GPUI layout with native view bounds on the main thread. Verify
   Retina coordinate conversion, clipping, scrolling, resizing, window activation,
   full screen, and movement between displays. Preserve hit testing, focus,
   keyboard navigation, window dragging, and accessibility semantics.
4. Give the material ownership of its appearance. Reusing the theme's 80–86%
   tint over native glass could obscure the effect; tune that integration
   separately. Rounded/floating chrome also needs workspace layout changes,
   rather than a new color in `extension.toml` or theme JSON.
5. Honor system appearance, Reduce Transparency, Increase Contrast, and Reduce
   Motion through native APIs and change notifications. Supply solid surfaces
   when required, and keep the existing rendering path on other platforms.
   See [NSWorkspace accessibility settings](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appkit/nsworkspace).

If native composition cannot support the intended layout, a GPUI-rendered
approximation is a separate approach: it would need an available backdrop texture,
additional render passes, masks, and blur/refraction shading. It would not be
Apple's native material and would require its own performance and accessibility
work. Do not treat a custom shader as proof that native glass works.

### Evidence required before calling it supported

Build and run the prototype on macOS 27. Confirm the intended backdrop actually
participates in the effect, foreground text stays sharp, and controls remain
usable. Review light/dark appearance, busy/quiet backdrops, accessibility changes,
inactive windows, full screen, and different display scales. Profile typing,
scrolling, resizing, frame pacing, GPU cost, and memory against unmodified Zed.
Restore the fallback and check unsupported OS/platform builds. Whole-window blur
working, JSON schema passing, or a screenshot alone is insufficient evidence.

The reported [macOS 27 blur issue #64284](https://github.com/zed-industries/zed/issues/64284)
must be reproduced against the chosen Zed revision before diagnosing it. Fixing
that blur path and introducing native Liquid Glass are separate changes.

Source/API review: 22 October 2026. No native glass prototype has been built here.

## Release

Version 0.5.0 is the local macOS Glass release under adeze's maintenance.
The extension ID is `macos-glass-theme`; repository metadata points to this project.
The earlier local ID was `macos-glass`. Reinstall the development extension when
testing this manifest so Zed registers the new ID; appearance names are unchanged.
CI validates the theme, preset, and submission metadata; it does not publish.
Follow [publishing/submission.md](publishing/submission.md) only after release is
explicitly authorized. Creating a tag alone does not publish this extension.
